{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head 23a112f084e12ca0300f90f2a43eefc176a956d3, resting on all 47 submitted artifacts, notes/2026-10-01-laneB-git.md first.\n\nThe ledger is a substantial improvement: unanswered fork/mint steps survive restart, empty inventories do not immediately settle them, and a post-mint sweep now removes the applied-before-error token before returning ready. I independently confirmed the former duplicate-token reproduction now leaves exactly one live token. The earlier lease fencing, pending renewal deadline and bounded diff fixes remain intact. Two completion rules still allow cleanup to disappear before an outstanding remote step applies.\n\n1. P1 — a rejected RPC is not necessarily a remote completion fence (workspaces.ts answered(), mintWithinLease catch, and ensureFork finally). Every thrown error marks the step answered, including a connection/transport failure with no remote answer. cleanFork can then settle the step against an absent repository or empty token snapshot. In an independent real-SQLite fake-binding test, fork(name) schedules its remote creation but throws TypeError(\"connection lost after request sent\") immediately. Provisioning fails; reconcile returns 0 and nextDue is null, with fork-create doneReason \"no-repository\". Releasing the remote work afterward creates the fork and its live 24-hour creation token; later reconcile still returns 0 and leaves the token active. This reproduction applies before the configured bound, so retaining a larger bound alone cannot fix it. The RepoHandle error contract does not distinguish remote completion from transport failure.\n\nKeep ambiguous failures in flight. Only a positively identified terminal remote response with a documented completion guarantee may mark a step answered; otherwise preserve scheduled inventory. Record each retry attempt separately when an earlier failed attempt can still apply, including fork retries, rather than treating the final response as completion of every attempt. Add transport-failure-before-late-apply tests for fork and mint, as well as the existing applied-before-error success cases.\n\n2. P1 — elapsed time based on an admitted assumption ends cleanup without evidence (beginStep/retire). Default applyBoundMs is one hour and the documentation expressly says this is not an Artifacts guarantee. Adding a token's TTL to an unproven last-apply time does not prove that no token can subsequently be created. I paused fork creation, restarted on the same SQL, advanced the injected clock just beyond 25 hours, and reconciled: the duty became done/\"past-bound\", pendingCleanup 0, nextDue null. The paused remote creation then ran and minted a fresh live 24-hour token, which subsequent reconcile never discovered. No real wait or live API was involved. This is the previous outstanding-write problem moved to a later timer.\n\nRetain uncertain duties and continue capped-backoff inventories unless a real remote fence or an enforceable, documented maximum apply time is available. A deployment-provided bound can be supported only with an explicit verified adapter guarantee and a conservative default; an arbitrary caller-selected/default duration must not be treated as proof. Actual known token expiry can retire that token's debt, but cannot retire an unresolved request that may mint another token later. Keep readiness for a newer lease independent from this background cleanup, as the candidate already does.\n\nArchitecture: the in-flight/owed/done ledger and post-install sweep are sound, and the one-live-host lock remains advisory coordination rather than restart evidence. The answered state must mean the remote operation is known finished; a local Promise rejection and a guessed duration cannot establish that. These workspace findings do not alter the landing engine's correct rule that token revocation and elapsed time never release an unknown publication slot.\n\nSecurity: the new sweep closes the demonstrated